WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:17.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello everyone its Peter Brown reading  Heidi Kemp's review of Dragon Quest  eleven echoes of an elusive age the  Dragon Quest series is a standard-bearer  for an entire genre numerous JRPGs that  have come and gone over the years have  adapted from and built upon many of the  formulas Dragon Quest established in the  fitt era while series like Final Fantasy  have transformed dramatically over time  Dragon Quest tends towards  traditionalism keeping many of its core  gameplay and story concepts from game to  game Dragon Quest 11 is no exception the  chosen hero and his growing group of  party members go on a globe spanning  adventure in a realm of fantasy and  magic exploring dungeons solving story  beats to proceed and battling foes in  turn-based combat it's a tale you're  probably familiar with if you've played  any classic Japanese RPG but Dragon  Quest 11 is proof that traditions and  tropes don't have to feel worn-out and  dull as this gorgeous adventure will  challenge your skills tug at your  heartstrings and keep you eagerly  playing further and further into its  lengthy quest fishing for rumors huh  some things never change the hero of  Dragon Quest xi is a reincarnation of  the luminary a chosen hero who will  destroy a foretold dark presence yet the  glorious hero is not welcomed with open  arms when his destiny is revealed some  fear han is the harbinger that darkness  is soon to come wishing him dead as the  hero adventures from his hometown into  the wider world  he makes many friends and faces many  enemies encounters wicked monsters  endures intense tribulations and fights  for light in the shadow of an empire  manipulated by darkness that setup  likely sounds quite familiar the story  and Dragon Quest 11 doesn't really offer  any novel plot beats or twists but  that's not a knock against it the game  does a spectacular job of melding  familiar story elements with engaging  characters an excellent choreography for  example a tournament sequence is  something you see a lot of in Japanese  RPGs but the presentation  characterization and sprinkling of humor  present and Dragon Quest Elevens  tournament arc makes it truly memorable  hello candle penitence  this is no ordinary and national one  unforgettable sequence involves the hero  learning about his mysterious origins  and the land that he came from while  that sounds like nothing more than a  genre cliche the way it's presented here  is absolutely beautiful and  heart-wrenching the characterization of  the games various personalities helps a  lot in making the story in world as  engaging as it is every member of your  party has a well-developed unique  personality that accentuates their role  in the story for example one of the more  memorable party members is a flamboyant  entertainer named Silvan doe his  over-the-top mannerisms cheerful  attitude and combat prowess make him  stand out but you also get the sense  that the clown act might be lies  something buried in his past there are  plenty of fun and pcs you'll interact  with in the story as well such as a love  starved mermaid an eccentric Dean of an  elite girls prep school and a stunningly  incompetent Prince finally the villains  like the conflicted Sir Hendrik and the  cunning jasper present a constant threat  that pushes you ever forward  [Music]  special praise should be given to the  games of localization while it does  change quite a few characters and place  names from the Japanese version it does  a magnificent job of making the dialogue  and overall mood of the game feel warm  so full and inviting character  personalities in the flavor of various  regions of the world come through and  dialogue with interesting flourishes  like accents and spacing I feel for the  editor who had to write hundreds of  dialogue boxes for the character who  only speaks in haiku even the incidental  menu in combat text has a fun  light-hearted feel to it that simply  makes running through menus more lively  and when things get somber and serious  the writing changes to match knowing  full well what sort of tone needs to be  [Music]  trained quest 11 is a very linear game  you hit one story point solve whatever  problem you're facing be it defeating a  monster or collecting an item beating a  minigame or various combinations of  these things and then you venture out to  the next area where you're presented  with a new story beat slashing down mobs  of enemies along the way to build up  your characters levels you can go off  the beaten path a bit to complete sub  quests and explore optional areas but  most locales are completely locked off  until you hit a specific point in the  story and like the world Dragon Quest xi  Kombat is familiar and traditional but  presented in a charming and engaging way  that makes it feel anything but dated  characters and enemies take individual  turns based on their agility and you  choose what characters do by either  picking commands from a text-based menu  or setting the CPU to act based on  preset guidelines animations play out as  blows are exchanged and spells are cast  and every so often there's a funky  little twist to the fight that liven  things up such as characters achieving a  pepped up state that raises their  abilities and grants them access to  special attacks while there's a setting  in the options that allows you to  physically move characters during battle  rather than having them stand in a  stationary row it doesn't change the  combat significantly positioning doesn't  affect attacks and the fighting remains  strictly turned driven boss battles  aren't terribly common but the big  fights are truly trying challenging you  to make use of your learned spells and  skills against a foe that will utterly  wipe you out if you don't play  strategically  [Music]  despite Dragon Quest 11 s massive length  anywhere from 60 to over a hundred hours  depending on how you pace yourself and  how much extra content in questing you  do it rarely feels like it's dragging  its feet there's always a new place to  explore a new character to encounter or  a new threat to tackle just around every  story corner occasionally the game gets  a bit murky when it comes to telling you  where to go next  in particular a mid game sequence  involving looking for six magical orbs  winds up being very confusing but it  doesn't often keep you in one place or  dealing with one subplot for too long  you also won't have to grind if you're  smart about picking enemy fights and  giving up character skill points and if  you ever need a bit of a break you can  invest time in various minigames like  crafting items horse racing and a casino  with slots and poker among other games  [Music]  innovation in games has talked about a  lot but it's also great to see  traditional gameplay formulas that have  been around for decades presented  exceptionally well Dragon Quest 11 is  one of the best modern examples of this  it's beautiful presentation both visual  and story-wise combines with the tried  and true gameplay formula for a game  that's full of heart and soul once you  find yourself sucked into the world of  Dragon Quest 11 it's going to be very  hard to put down until you reach the  grand finale  [Music]

